{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## THREE independent orphans reaped tonight, all from the pattern this ticket documents\n\nField evidence gathered while other work ran on this host. Each was found at PPID 1, each burning\nclose to a full core, and each is the `trap 'kill 0'` failure mode this ticket exists to correct:\n\n1. `zsh` wrapper at **87% CPU** (recorded earlier in this session).\n2. `zsh -c \"trap 'kill 0' EXIT INT TERM; sleep 1\"` at **94% CPU — 61 CPU-MINUTES on a\n   one-second sleep.**\n3. `pid 12699 /bin/zsh /tmp/exp6.sh` at PPID 1, **1.0 hour of CPU.**\n\nItem 2 is the sharpest illustration available: a command whose entire intended runtime is ONE\nSECOND accumulated sixty-one minutes of CPU, because the `trap 'kill 0'` that was supposed to\nbound it does not reap the wrapper it runs inside. The bound was written, looked correct, and did\nnothing.\n\n## Why this strengthens the ticket beyond the original premise\n\nThe ticket's premise is that `timeout` is absent on macOS (exit 127, silent no-op). These three\nshow the SECOND half of the guidance fails independently: even where an agent correctly skips\n`timeout` and reaches for the documented `trap 'kill 0' EXIT INT TERM` sweep, the sweep does\nnot reap a wrapper process. So an agent following AGENTS.md faithfully, on this host, gets NO\nbound from either mechanism.\n\n## The measurement consequence, which is the expensive part\n\nAll three were skewing timing-sensitive work while they ran. One implementing agent's\n`make verify` was killed at its wall-clock limit with three suites running concurrently; another\nreported its full-suite timings against a box that had a hidden full core consumed. A leaked\nprocess does not merely waste CPU — it silently corrupts the measurements taken during its\nlifetime, including the measurements of the very investigations that spawn them.\n\n## For the fix\n\nThis is corroboration, not new scope: the ACs already require guidance that fails LOUDLY rather\nthan silently, and that names the `trap 'kill 0'` subshell limitation. These three cases are\nworth citing in the guidance itself as the reason the limitation is called out — a reader who\nknows a bound can look correct and do nothing is far more likely to verify it than one who is\nmerely told to add a trap."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788554034548266001,"uuid":"28dba8ec-66db-4110-a199-1eb0d78e7500"}